\citation{zhaoAPL2007,keplingerPNAS2010}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Electromechanical neutral-stability curves (perturbation analysis) at pre-compression $\lambda _{pre}=0.9$ ($\varepsilon ^{pre}=0.1$): normalized nominal electric field $\tilde  E\sqrt  {\epsilon /\mu }$ versus normalized wavelength $\ell /H_f$ for several elastocapillary numbers $\gamma /(\mu H_f)$. The critical field is the minimum of each curve, which rises and moves to longer wavelength as surface tension increases (Figure\nobreakspace  {}8\hbox {}).\relax }}{12}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:e2_elec}{{6}{12}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The same electromechanical neutral-stability curves at pre-\emph  {stretch} $\lambda _{pre}=1.2$. As in the pre-compressed case, increasing surface tension raises the critical field and lengthens the selected wavelength; the absolute fields are uniformly lower than at $\lambda _{pre}=0.9$ (Figure\nobreakspace  {}6\hbox {}) because the thinner pre-stretched film reaches a given true field $E_2=\lambda _{pre}\tilde  E$ at a smaller applied voltage.\relax }}{13}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:e2_elec_stretch}{{7}{13}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces No pre-strain ($\lambda _{pre}=1$): critical nominal field $E_c\sqrt  {\epsilon /\mu }$ versus elastocapillary number $\gamma /(\mu H_f)$, extended to $\gamma /(\mu H_f)=20$. The linear perturbation criterion (solid line) gives the smooth wrinkle threshold, which grows as $\sqrt  {\gamma /(\mu H_f)}$. Nonlinear finite-element onsets (markers) lie below it: the FE resolves the subcritical crease, which nucleates before the smooth wrinkle. The gap narrows as surface tension suppresses the crease.\relax }}{13}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:lam1_compare}{{8}{13}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Pre-strain dependence at $\bar  \gamma =2$: finite element (markers) and perturbation theory (line), swept from pre-compression $\lambda _{pre}=0.8$ to pre-stretch $\lambda _{pre}=1.3$. (a) The nominal critical field $\tilde  E_c\sqrt  {\epsilon /\mu }$ rises with pre-compression and falls with pre-stretch. (b) The true field $E_{2,c}=\lambda _{pre}\tilde  E_c$ is nearly $\lambda $-independent (FE to within a few percent; theory to within ${\sim }10\%$), the signature of the kinematic-thinning mechanism. The constant vertical offset between FE and theory is the crease-to-wrinkle gap of Figure\nobreakspace  {}8\hbox {}.\relax }}{14}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:em_validation}{{9}{14}}
